.SH NAME
.PP
yaesm - a backup tool with support for multiple file systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]yaesm\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]SUBCOMMAND\f[R]
[\f[I]SUBCOMMAND-OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Yaesm creates and schedules filesystem backups.
Backups may stay on the local system, be sent to a remote system, or be
pulled from a remote system.
Backup schedules, retention limits, and storage locations are defined in
a YAML configuration file.
.PP
Yaesm may be run interactively or continuously as a scheduler under an
init system.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.PP
Global options apply to every subcommand and must appear before the
subcommand.
Running \f[B]yaesm --help\f[R] shows the global help; running a
subcommand with \f[B]--help\f[R] shows help for that subcommand.
.PP
\f[B]-h\f[R], \f[B]--help\f[R]
.PP
Show help and exit.
.PP
\f[B]--version\f[R]
.PP
Show the installed yaesm version and exit.
.PP
\f[B]-c\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], \f[B]--config\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PP
Read configuration from \f[I]FILE\f[R].
The default is \f[V]/etc/yaesm/config.yaml\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]--log-level\f[R] \f[I]LEVEL\f[R]
.PP
Set the minimum logging level.
Valid values are \f[B]DEBUG\f[R], \f[B]INFO\f[R], \f[B]WARNING\f[R],
\f[B]ERROR\f[R], and \f[B]CRITICAL\f[R].
The default is \f[B]INFO\f[R].
At \f[B]DEBUG\f[R], every external command executed by yaesm is also
logged.
.PP
\f[B]--log-stderr\f[R]
.PP
Log to standard error.
.PP
\f[B]--log-file\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PP
Append logs to \f[I]FILE\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]--log-syslog\f[R][=\f[I]ADDRESS\f[R]]
.PP
Log to syslog.
\f[I]ADDRESS\f[R] is the Unix socket path and defaults to
\f[V]/dev/log\f[R].
When this option immediately precedes a subcommand, use
\f[V]--log-syslog=/dev/log\f[R] to avoid treating the subcommand as
\f[I]ADDRESS\f[R].
.PP
More than one logging destination may be selected.
If none is selected, yaesm logs to standard error.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS backup
.PP
\f[B]yaesm\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[B]backup\f[R]
\f[I]BACKUP\f[R][,\f[I]BACKUP\f[R]...]
[\f[B]--keep\f[R] \f[I]COUNT\f[R]]
.PP
Create one or more immediate backups.
Multiple configured backup names may be given as a comma-separated list.
The names are validated before any backups begin, then the backups run
sequentially in the requested order.
If one fails, the remaining backups still run and the command returns a
failure status.
.PP
Immediate backups use each selected backup\[aq]s configured backend.
Their retention is managed separately from scheduled timeframes.
.PP
\f[B]--keep\f[R] \f[I]COUNT\f[R]
.PP
For each selected backup, keep no more than \f[I]COUNT\f[R] immediate
backups, including the newly created backup.
\f[I]COUNT\f[R] must be a positive integer.
Immediate backups are kept indefinitely when this option is omitted.
.SS check
.PP
\f[B]yaesm\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[B]check\f[R]
[\f[I]BACKUP\f[R][,\f[I]BACKUP\f[R]...]] [\f[B]-q\f[R] |
\f[B]--quiet\f[R]]
.PP
Check whether configured backups meet the requirements needed to run.
With no backup names, all configured backups are checked.
Multiple names may be given as a comma-separated list.
The names are validated before any checks begin.
.PP
By default, every check is shown as \f[B]PASS\f[R] or \f[B]FAIL\f[R],
followed by any error messages.
The command succeeds only if every selected backup passes all of its
checks.
If all checks pass, the backups are very likely to run successfully,
though later runtime failures are still possible.
.PP
\f[B]-q\f[R], \f[B]--quiet\f[R]
.PP
Show only failed checks and their error messages.
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ yaesm check
$ yaesm check home-backup
$ yaesm check home-backup,root-backup --quiet
\f[R]
.fi
.SS find
.PP
\f[B]yaesm\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[B]find\f[R]
\f[I]BACKUP\f[R][,\f[I]BACKUP\f[R]...]
[\f[I]QUERY\f[R]...]
[\f[B]--query\f[R] \f[I]QUERY\f[R]...]
[\f[B]--timeframe\f[R] \f[I]TIMEFRAME\f[R][,\f[I]TIMEFRAME\f[R]...]]
.PP
Print the locations of existing backups.
For each configured backup, results are printed from newest to oldest.
Multiple backup names may be given as a comma-separated list.
Each result is printed as a backup locator.
Its format depends on the backend and storage location.
.PP
With no query, all matching backups are printed.
Finding no matching backups is not an error.
.PP
The following queries are supported:
.PP
\f[B]all\f[R]
.PP
Select every backup.
This is the default.
.PP
\f[B]newest\f[R]
.PP
Select the newest backup.
.PP
\f[B]oldest\f[R]
.PP
Select the oldest backup.
.PP
\f[B]after\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R]
.PP
Select backups newer than \f[I]TIME\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]before\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R]
.PP
Select backups older than \f[I]TIME\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]between\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R]
.PP
Select backups between the two times, including the endpoints.
The times may be given in either order.
.PP
\f[B]closest\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R]
.PP
Select the backup closest to \f[I]TIME\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]TIME\f[R] may have one of these forms:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]now-Nm\f[R], \f[V]now-Nh\f[R], or \f[V]now-Nd\f[R] for a positive
number of minutes, hours, or days before the current time
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]YYYY-MM-DD\f[R] for midnight on a date
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM\f[R] for a date and time
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]HH:MM\f[R] for a time today
.PP
\f[B]-q\f[R] \f[I]QUERY\f[R], \f[B]--query\f[R] \f[I]QUERY\f[R]
.PP
Add another query.
This option may be repeated.
Results from multiple queries are combined without duplicates.
.PP
\f[B]-t\f[R] \f[I]TIMEFRAME\f[R], \f[B]--timeframe\f[R]
\f[I]TIMEFRAME\f[R], \f[B]--timeframes\f[R] \f[I]TIMEFRAME\f[R]
.PP
Limit results to one or more timeframes.
Values may be comma-separated and the option may be repeated.
Valid values are \f[B]5minute\f[R], \f[B]hourly\f[R], \f[B]daily\f[R],
\f[B]weekly\f[R], \f[B]monthly\f[R], \f[B]yearly\f[R], and
\f[B]immediate\f[R].
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ yaesm find home-backup
$ yaesm find home-backup,root-backup newest
$ yaesm find home-backup after now-7d --timeframes daily,weekly
$ yaesm find home-backup before 2026-08-01T12:00
$ yaesm find home-backup between 2026-08-01 2026-08-15
$ yaesm find home-backup closest 12:30
$ yaesm find home-backup,root-backup --query oldest --query newest
$ yaesm find home-backup --timeframe hourly --timeframe daily
\f[R]
.fi
.SS run
.PP
\f[B]yaesm\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[B]run\f[R] [\f[B]--lockfile\f[R]
\f[I]FILE\f[R]]
.PP
Start the backup scheduler and continue running until it is stopped.
Every configured timeframe for every backup is scheduled.
This command is intended to be managed by an init system, but it may
also be run directly in a terminal.
.PP
Only one scheduler may use a given lock file.
If the lock cannot be acquired, the command reports an error and exits
with a failure status.
\f[B]SIGINT\f[R] and \f[B]SIGTERM\f[R] stop the scheduler gracefully and
wait for running backups to finish.
.PP
\f[B]SIGHUP\f[R] reloads the configuration file that yaesm was started
with.
Backups that are already running are allowed to complete using the old
configuration.
Future backups use the new configuration.
If the configuration is invalid, yaesm reports the errors and keeps the
current schedule.
.PP
\f[B]--lockfile\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PP
Use \f[I]FILE\f[R] as the scheduler lock file.
The default is \f[V]/run/lock/yaesm-run.lock\f[R].
The file is created if needed, but its parent directory must already
exist and be writable by the running user.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
The configuration file is the heart of yaesm.
It defines the backups and drives how they are created, scheduled,
checked, found, and retained.
Yaesm reads its YAML configuration from \f[V]/etc/yaesm/config.yaml\f[R]
by default.
Use \f[B]--config\f[R] to select another file.
Configuration files use YAML 1.1.
.PP
The top level of the file must be a nonempty mapping defining one or
more backups.
Each entry consists of a backup name, a configured backend, and a list
of timeframes.
Backend-specific settings determine how and where its backups are
stored.
.PP
Yaesm provides no global backup settings or inheritance; each backup is
configured and validated independently.
Unknown settings are rejected.
Yaesm reports configuration errors before running the selected
subcommand.
.PP
Backup names must begin with an ASCII letter.
The remaining characters may be ASCII letters, digits, hyphens,
underscores, colons, or \f[V]\[at]\f[R] signs.
.PP
Apart from its name, every backup accepts two common settings:
.PP
\f[B]backend\f[R]
.PP
Required.
The backend used to create and manage this backup.
The currently supported values are \f[B]btrfs\f[R] and \f[B]rsync\f[R].
Backend names are case-sensitive.
See BACKENDS for backend-specific settings, requirements, behavior, and
examples.
.PP
\f[B]timeframes\f[R]
.PP
Required.
A list containing any of \f[B]5minute\f[R], \f[B]hourly\f[R],
\f[B]daily\f[R], \f[B]weekly\f[R], \f[B]monthly\f[R], or
\f[B]yearly\f[R].
Each selected timeframe requires the corresponding settings described
under TIMEFRAMES.
Timeframe names are case-sensitive and each should appear only once.
An empty list creates no scheduled jobs, but the backup may still be run
manually.
The \f[B]immediate\f[R] timeframe is reserved for manual backups and is
not valid here.
.SS Timeframes
.PP
Timeframes control when scheduled backups run and how many backups are
retained.
Every \f[V]*_keep\f[R] setting must be an integer greater than zero.
After a successful backup, yaesm retains at most that many backups for
the same backup and timeframe.
Retention for manual backups is controlled separately by \f[B]yaesm
backup --keep\f[R].
.PP
All schedules use the local time zone of the system running yaesm.
Values in a \f[V]*_times\f[R] list use 24-hour \f[V]HH:MM\f[R] notation.
Hours range from 00 through 23 and minutes from 00 through 59.
Quote these values so YAML always treats them as times rather than
numbers.
Every selected list of minutes, times, or days must contain at least one
value.
.PP
Each timeframe is scheduled independently.
If two timeframes for the same backup select the same time, both jobs
may run concurrently.
Choose different times when concurrent runs are not wanted.
.SS 5minute
.PP
Run every five minutes.
.PP
\f[B]5minute_keep\f[R]
.PP
Required positive integer specifying how many five-minute backups to
retain.
.SS hourly
.PP
Run every hour at each selected minute.
.PP
\f[B]hourly_keep\f[R]
.PP
Required positive integer specifying how many hourly backups to retain.
.PP
\f[B]hourly_minutes\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more integer minute values from 0 through 59.
.SS daily
.PP
Run every day at each selected time.
.PP
\f[B]daily_keep\f[R]
.PP
Required positive integer specifying how many daily backups to retain.
.PP
\f[B]daily_times\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more quoted \f[V]HH:MM\f[R] times.
.SS weekly
.PP
Run on every combination of the selected weekdays and times.
.PP
\f[B]weekly_keep\f[R]
.PP
Required positive integer specifying how many weekly backups to retain.
.PP
\f[B]weekly_times\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more quoted \f[V]HH:MM\f[R] times.
.PP
\f[B]weekly_days\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more weekday names: \f[B]monday\f[R],
\f[B]tuesday\f[R], \f[B]wednesday\f[R], \f[B]thursday\f[R],
\f[B]friday\f[R], \f[B]saturday\f[R], or \f[B]sunday\f[R].
Names are case-insensitive.
.SS monthly
.PP
Run on every combination of the selected days and times.
A day that does not exist in a particular month is skipped for that
month.
.PP
\f[B]monthly_keep\f[R]
.PP
Required positive integer specifying how many monthly backups to retain.
.PP
\f[B]monthly_times\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more quoted \f[V]HH:MM\f[R] times.
.PP
\f[B]monthly_days\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more integer days from 1 through 31.
.SS yearly
.PP
Run on every combination of the selected days and times.
Days use their position in a non-leap year: 1 is January 1 and 365 is
December 31.
Day 366 is not supported.
.PP
\f[B]yearly_keep\f[R]
.PP
Required positive integer specifying how many yearly backups to retain.
.PP
\f[B]yearly_times\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more quoted \f[V]HH:MM\f[R] times.
.PP
\f[B]yearly_days\f[R]
.PP
Required list of one or more integer days from 1 through 365.
.SH BACKENDS
.PP
A backend determines how backups are created, located, checked, and
removed.
Each backend accepts its own settings and has its own system
requirements.
See CONFIGURATION for backup names and timeframes.
.SS SSH settings
.PP
Backends that connect to remote systems over SSH may share the following
settings.
.PP
\f[B]ssh_key\f[R]
.PP
Required when a backend uses an SSH target.
This must be an absolute path to an existing local private-key file used
for SSH authentication.
.PP
\f[B]ssh_config\f[R]
.PP
Optional.
An absolute path to an existing local OpenSSH configuration file.
When set, the file is passed to OpenSSH with \f[B]-F\f[R].
.SS Path-based backends
.PP
The \f[B]btrfs\f[R] and \f[B]rsync\f[R] backends operate on local or
remote directory paths.
They share the following path settings and use the SSH settings above
when a path is remote:
.PP
\f[B]src_dir\f[R]
.PP
Required.
The directory to back up.
This may be an absolute path to an existing local directory or an SSH
target specification.
.PP
\f[B]dst_dir\f[R]
.PP
Required.
The directory in which backups are stored.
This may be an absolute path to an existing local directory or an SSH
target specification.
Yaesm creates backups inside this directory.
.PP
Local paths must begin with \f[V]/\f[R] and must already exist when the
configuration is read.
\f[V]\[ti]\f[R] and environment variables are not expanded.
.SS Remote sources and destinations
.PP
For path-based backends, an SSH target has this form:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ssh://[pPORT:][USER\[at]]HOST:/ABSOLUTE/PATH
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The \f[V]p\f[R] before \f[I]PORT\f[R] is literal.
\f[I]USER\f[R] and \f[I]PORT\f[R] are optional.
\f[I]HOST\f[R] may be a hostname or an alias from the selected OpenSSH
configuration.
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ssh://backup.example:/srv/backups
ssh://backup\[at]backup.example:/srv/backups
ssh://p2222:backup\[at]backup.example:/srv/backups
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If both \f[B]src_dir\f[R] and \f[B]dst_dir\f[R] are remote, they must
use the same SSH user, host, and port.
The backup then runs on that system without transferring its data
through the system running Yaesm.
An explicit \f[B]ssh_key\f[R] is required even if the key is also named
in the OpenSSH configuration.
Authentication must work without interactive input, and the remote host
key must already be trusted.
.PP
Yaesm rejects invalid SSH target syntax and invalid local key or
configuration files.
Run \f[B]yaesm check\f[R] to test the connection, remote directory, and
backend requirements.
.SS btrfs
.PP
The \f[B]btrfs\f[R] backend accepts the shared path settings above.
The source and destination must be on btrfs filesystems, and the
\f[V]btrfs\f[R] command must be available wherever yaesm operates on
them.
.PP
\f[B]btrfs_bootstrap_refresh\f[R]
.PP
Optional positive integer specifying the maximum age, in days, of the
bootstrap snapshot used for incremental transfers.
A stale bootstrap is recreated without removing existing backups.
.SS rsync
.PP
The \f[B]rsync\f[R] backend accepts the shared path settings above.
The \f[V]rsync\f[R] command must be available wherever yaesm operates on
the source or destination.
.PP
\f[B]rsync_extra_opts\f[R]
.PP
Optional rsync options, given as a string or a list of strings.
Each value is split on whitespace and passed to rsync in addition to
yaesm\[aq]s default options.
.SS Examples
.PP
A local rsync backup using every configurable timeframe:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
home-backup:
  backend: rsync
  src_dir: /home
  dst_dir: /srv/backups
  timeframes:
    - 5minute
    - hourly
    - daily
    - weekly
    - monthly
    - yearly
  5minute_keep: 12
  hourly_keep: 24
  hourly_minutes: [1]
  daily_keep: 7
  daily_times: [\[dq]02:02\[dq]]
  weekly_keep: 4
  weekly_times: [\[dq]03:03\[dq]]
  weekly_days: [sunday]
  monthly_keep: 12
  monthly_times: [\[dq]04:04\[dq]]
  monthly_days: [1]
  yearly_keep: 5
  yearly_times: [\[dq]05:06\[dq]]
  yearly_days: [1]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
A btrfs backup sent to a remote destination:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
offsite-home-backup:
  backend: btrfs
  src_dir: /home
  dst_dir: ssh://p2222:backup\[at]backup.example:/srv/backups
  ssh_key: /root/.ssh/yaesm
  ssh_config: /root/.ssh/config
  timeframes: [daily]
  daily_keep: 7
  daily_times: [\[dq]02:00\[dq]]
\f[R]
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\f[B]0\f[R]
.PP
The command completed successfully.
This also applies when \f[B]find\f[R] has no matches and when help or
version information is requested.
.PP
\f[B]1\f[R]
.PP
The requested operation failed, a check failed, the scheduler could not
run, or yaesm encountered an unexpected error.
.PP
\f[B]2\f[R]
.PP
The command line was invalid, or \f[B]check\f[R] or \f[B]find\f[R]
received an invalid backup selection or query.
.PP
\f[B]78\f[R]
.PP
Yaesm detected a configuration error before running the subcommand.
